    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the degree measure of an angle whose measure is double the measure of its complement, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Define the angle and its complement:**\n   Let the measure of the angle be \\( x \\) degrees.\n   The complement of the angle is \\( 90^\\circ - x \\) degrees.\n\n2. **Set up the relationship:**\n   According to the problem, the measure of the angle is double the measure of its complement. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n   \\[\n   x = 2 \\times (90^\\circ - x)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve the equation:**\n   First, distribute the 2 on the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   x = 180^\\circ - 2x\n   \\]\n   Next, add \\( 2x \\) to both sides to combine the \\( x \\) terms:\n   \\[\n   x + 2x = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Simplify the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   3x = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Finally, divide both sides by 3 to solve for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} = 60^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   The measure of the angle is \\( 60^\\circ \\).\n\nTherefore, the degree measure of the angle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
