      "text": "Let's denote the measure of the angle by \\( x \\) degrees. The measure of its complement is \\( 90^\\circ - x \\) degrees. According to the problem, the measure of the angle is double the measure of its complement. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[ x = 2(90^\\circ - x) \\]\n\nNext, we will solve this equation step by step.\n\n1. Distribute the 2 on the right side of the equation:\n\\[ x = 180^\\circ - 2x \\]\n\n2. Add \\( 2x \\) to both sides of the equation to isolate the \\( x \\) terms on one side:\n\\[ x + 2x = 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 3x = 180^\\circ \\]\n\n3. Divide both sides by 3 to solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = 60^\\circ \\]\n\nSo, the measure of the angle is \\( 60^\\circ \\). Let's verify this solution by checking that the angle's measure is double its complement.\n\nThe complement of \\( 60^\\circ \\) is \\( 90^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 30^\\circ \\). Double the complement is \\( 2 \\times 30^\\circ = 60^\\circ \\), which matches the angle's measure.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{60}\n\\]",
